# =============================================================================
# AquariusOS — the MSI Claw 8 AI+'s "the controller may not wake the machine"
# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS IS, IN PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# The Claw's built-in controller is wired to the computer over an internal USB
# connection, exactly like a gamepad you would plug in. Linux lets a USB device
# wake a sleeping computer, and a gamepad bolted to the sides of a machine that
# lives in a bag is the worst possible thing to give that power to: a thumbstick
# nudged by a jacket wakes the handheld, the screen comes on inside the bag, and
# the battery is gone by the time you get there.
#
# Worse, it can wake the machine WHILE it is still going to sleep, which leaves
# it half-suspended — neither awake nor asleep, fans on, nothing on screen.
#
# This rule tells Linux that this one USB device is not allowed to wake
# anything. Putting the handheld to sleep with the power button, or from Steam,
# still works exactly as before, and the power button still wakes it.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THREE PRODUCT NUMBERS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Claw's controller changes its USB identity depending on which mode its own
# little chip is in. All of them are the same physical pad:
#
#     0db0:1901   XInput mode   — the one AquariusOS expects, and the one to set
#                                 in MSI Center M in Windows before wiping it
#     0db0:1902   DInput mode   — an older style of gamepad
#     0db0:1903   Desktop mode  — the pad pretends to be a mouse and keyboard
#
# (There is a fourth, 0db0:1904, which only appears while the machine is in its
# BIOS screen. Nothing is asleep then, so it is not listed.)
#
# On Fedora's kernel 7.2 nothing on Linux can switch between these modes — the
# driver that does it (`hid-msi`) arrives in kernel 7.3. So the rule covers all
# three, and whichever one the pad happens to be in is covered.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS OURS, AND IT IS UNTESTED UNTIL THE BENCH
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The equivalent rule for the ROG Ally is copied byte-for-byte from Bazzite,
# which has years of mileage on that hardware. There is no such rule for the
# Claw anywhere: no distribution ships one. This file is AquariusOS's own, built
# on the same idea and the same syntax as the Ally rule, and it has never run on
# a Claw. The first bench session is what turns it from a reasonable guess into
# a tested fact — the bench list in docs/restart/handheld-claw.md has the line
# for it ("a stick twitch does not wake it").
#
# If it turns out to cause trouble, deleting it changes nothing else: the
# machine simply goes back to letting the pad wake it.
#
# Plain-language guide: docs/restart/handheld-claw.md
# =============================================================================
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", DRIVER=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0db0", ATTR{idProduct}=="1901", ATTR{power/wakeup}="disabled"
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", DRIVER=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0db0", ATTR{idProduct}=="1902", ATTR{power/wakeup}="disabled"
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", DRIVER=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0db0", ATTR{idProduct}=="1903", ATTR{power/wakeup}="disabled"
